{"kind":"assert","text":"Correction to ruling 9cf4b1cd, on what a bind does while the old bundle serves. The four readings stand. The fallback does not.\n\nThe ruling says a bind under the old map is refused for missing authorization, changes nothing, and can be retried. The source at 8e9f0942 says otherwise:\n\n- bind-repository in repository-binding-cohort-data.ts has two guards: state unpublished, and binding-github-repository, which checks only the repository's format. Neither guard reads the host's binding map. The act commits under either bundle.\n- On commit it records the repository, moves the Destination from unpublished to empty, and opens the firstHead and mint operations.\n- Under the App-only bundle the map is empty, so bound() in github-wiring.ts returns false for this Destination. operations.ts:423 then leaves each attempt recorded and unsent. That part holds: no GitHub request leaves.\n- A second bind is refused, because the Destination is no longer unpublished.\n\nSo a bind under the old bundle is not a no-op. It spends the room's one unpublished-to-bound transition early. Per the comment at operations.ts:419-421, the walk after the next restart looks at those attempts again, so they would probably go out once the binding bundle serves. Nothing in the evidence shows that recovery.\n\nHand the room back only after all four readings hold. Then a bind that seems to stall is no reason to bind again: read the Destination first. If it shows the repository with operations open, wait for the walk.","body":{"to":"planner"}}